 a hospital I guess  they're expensive so I did what people  in the 60s did 20 of us moved into a six  bedroom house and said we were a  hospital open 24/7 and in the 12 years  we operated we had about 500 to a  thousand people in our home each month  with five 250 overnight guests a night  in a six bedroom house that already had  20 adults and its children living there  so let's say it was intimate yes and I  wanted a hospital setting that was happy  funny loving cooperative creative and  thoughtful that that's a climate for  good health and a climate for non Berna  because so often in wanting to change  the healthcare system it's more about  helping the patient's get what they need  rather than the staff gets what they  need so all of this time I'm in the 50th  year of fundraising for a project I  thought would take four to find the  funding because our design eliminated  90% of the cost of care that's what I  want to focus in on can you talk about  how ninety you know people talk about we  can eliminate 3% with some bureaucratic  simplification we can look at  unnecessary testing to get another 5%  how do you achieve a 90% reduction in  cost well put on your seat though yes  okay so  we have 327 acres in Pocahontas County  West Virginia the poorest state for  health care in the u.s. yes we want to  build a 40 bed primary care rural  community hospital on that land all of  the permanent staff would live there as  a communal eco village I'm thinking a  hundred and twenty permanent staff and  that everyone from the cleaning person  to the surgeon would make the same  salary hold your seat three hundred  dollars a month okay  the hospital will be an extensive  permaculture farm and artist colony and  I feel that we with that design we we  won't accept health insurance no one  will have to fill out any of those forms  and we'll we'll practice medicine an  interesting addition is that we we found  in the 12 years we were practice even  though we often did it illegally 