  that are primary care there are that are  tertiary care and so if if the doctor at  the surgeons we have feel comfortable  we'll do it and where do you think that  you will find the medical staff that is  interested in  in that salary of $3,600 a year  well a bonus of being in the 50th year  rather than the first year and adding to  that fame when we closed our doors in 83  within a year I felt a real intense  absence of care I need to care I'm one  of those people and I didn't have a  hospital and so I decide I can't go and  work in a regular hospital that that  would hurt me and so I did I added two  kinds of care what was Clown trips and  I've done clowned trips all over the  world in fact the last Russia trip that  we had had eight doctors and five nurses  because they wanted to put more humor in  their practice and the other thing I did  was answer mail so in these years I've  answered 400,000 letters from a hundred  and thirty nine countries well from all  over the world  I hear discontent in medical doctors and  in nurses that hate what they're doing  they hate the context they the hospitals  are unhappy they're miserable and this  is pretty well known in this country as  well and so you know one can decide that  rich has many meanings rich is good  friends hmm rich is doing exactly what  you want in the way that you want yes  rich is having good health are you  getting what I'm saying absolutely on  our land there are three waterfalls a  four acre lake black bear beaver otter  deer wild turkey it's it's a natural  paradise and you get to practice  medicine so as a family doctor I like my  initial interview with an adult to be 3  or 4 hours long because we're  complicated well and I want to  have so much time that we fall in love  with each other that the friendship  between health professional and patient  can often be the medicine they need  right and Pat you were portrayed by the  late Robin Williams in the film patch  Adams which is of course based based on  your life and your views on medicine  that that you've told us a little bit  about here how did you feel about that  portrayal do you feel that it was an  accurate portrayal of you well I loved  Robin his personality though he was much  more introverted than I he he was a  wonderful playmate and he cares so the  movie wanted to sell tickets they  promised to build us and did not and I  mean they changed what they wanted to  change so the real person murdered was  not my girlfriend but my closest male  friend in medical school that was the  thing I disliked the most the creative  liberties they took right they they  didn't want to change the future of  healthcare they wanted to sell a Robin  Williams movie and they did didn't they  oh it was a huge seller yeah I I spanked  their bottom that they did not build our  hospital so explain that they had made  an intimation that they would support  your hospital with some of the proceeds  build it they said we'll build the  hospital well you know Fame is a  nightmare and so I needed something and  by that time enough time had passed  where I wanted a hospital and I knew  that they would make enough to be rich  themselves and I wanted to hide it I  didn't want the money  I wanted a hospital and it did not  materialize right Wow well well yes yah  go ahead I asked  they didn't answer the letters they  didn't respond to that Wow that is that  is an incredible story  well dr. Patz yeah are you 90% of the  people who have said patch I'm gonna  send you some money didn't do it either  why do you think that is I think that's  the world we live in a sad reality you  know I've never been discouraged it's  really fun to work for something  beautiful they're great members of the  team and I'm having fun along the way  I've climbed at 10,000 deathbeds in  Trinidad I clown for five men who were  hung the next morning for capital crimes  or loved it because of our work groups  of clowns are going in hospitals in a  hundred and forty countries to try the  up the vibe I guess and so I'm not going  oh darn we haven't built right the  hospital may not have materialized but  so many other things have correct and  we're not the only people trying to  change health care delivery and and yet  it hasn't been changing much right  that's for sure that's for sure  listen dr. patch Adams it's been such a  pleasure speaking with you and you  sharing a little bit about your life and  what you've done I encourage my audience  to learn more about what you're doing  including at the gesundheit Institute  and I so appreciate your time today

